musician moby has called on the cop 26  climate change conference here in  glasgow to make the climate damaging  impacts of animal agriculture central to  the agenda if world leaders hope to make  meaningful pledges to avert catastrophic  climate breakdown and moby who's not  just a musician but also an animal  rights activist and also supporter of  the humane society international joins  me now from los angeles i'm delighted to  say great to see you  moby we all know don't we that animal  agriculture damages  the climate but you feel that that  message isn't getting heard enough here  in glasgow is that right  yeah well i mean remember cop 21 there  wasn't a single panel or mention  of the consequences of meat and dairy  production and i was hoping that things  would change for cop26 because obviously  public awareness has evolved  but once again you look at the  legislative agenda cop 26 and there's no  mention  of  almost no mention of animal agriculture  and methane production and rainforest  deforestation and the other incredibly  deleterious consequences of using meat  and dairy for food  so what would you like them to be  hearing then what would the message be  the message would be one of honesty  you know it reminds me of a conversation  i had with al gore  a few years ago  and i'm paraphrasing what vice president  gore said but he basically said the role  of meat and dairy production in climate  change is the true inconvenient truth  you know it's the truth that politicians  don't want to talk about that corporate  corporations don't want to talk about  it's the truth that so many people don't  want to address for so many different  reasons and my hope  is simply  that there will be a change and people  will be willing to look at the causes of  climate change  um it's international vegan day uh i  think today um one inconvenient truth is  that you're never going to tell people  that they can't eat meat you can't ban  meat altogether can you  well i don't know it's certainly i mean  if that were to is that what you'd like  to see would you like to see meat banned  uh what i would like to see first and  foremost is for me to cost what it  actually costs you know to factor in the  direct costs because  here in the united states and i assume  in the uk as well and in europe we  subsidize meat and dairy production so  much  that if it weren't for these subsidies a  family of four going to burger king  would spend about a hundred dollars  on a meal so i'm just saying remove the  subsidies stop subsidizing an industry  that is destroying human health and is  destroying the climate on the only home  that we have  um you say that the issue isn't being  heard at all  really here in glasgow but boris johnson  recently has said that he sees lab-grown  eco-friendly alternatives to meters as  being the future i mean you must welcome  that  i welcome anything  that puts us on a path  that will enable us to and i know this  might sound like hyperbole and i  apologize because it's not hyperbole i  applaud anything that will enable us to  avert catastrophe and at present i feel  like so many of us have our heads in the  sand like  you can't  fix a problem  if you're simply unwilling to look  at the empirically supported reasons why  the problem exists and so if it's lab  grown meat if it's  ending subsidies for meat and dairy  production whatever it takes to sort of  prevent this unfolding catastrophe  but of course it's not just meat eating  is it that causes uh huge issues with  the climate i was reading today about  asparagus one study has shown that for  every one kilogram of asparagus eaten in  the united kingdom 5.3 kilograms of  carbon dioxide is produced now that's no  good either is it  uh i mean all agriculture has  consequences there's no such thing i  mean all you could go even further and  be philosophical and say all actions  have consequences  of course  all agriculture requires water and  more often than not has co2  emissions but compared to meat and dairy  i mean even the united nations admits  that meat and dairy production is the  third leading cause of climate change so  i don't think asparagus  ranks as one of the global  leading causes for climate change and  keep in mind i'm a vegan and i don't  really like asparagus asparagus that  much i'm not going to stick up for us  yeah i mean that was just an example i  mean there are plenty more examples  aren't there of uh you know and  fertilizer and the way we farm crops and  all the rest of it um  how much of your campaign is actually  about um  the climate and the effects that meat  eating has on the climate and how much  is actually about the ethics of meat  eating because we know you're a huge  animal lover you do a huge amount of  work for animal welfare is it more about  that than actually about the climate  well in this instance especially with  cop26 happening now  my focus is on the climate i mean  personally  i'm a vegan and an animal rights  activist because i just can't in good  conscience be involved in anything that  causes animal suffering or contributes  to animal suffering but right now the  focus has to be on the climate and i  apologize for stating the obvious  but every issue that we care about  depends upon a stable climate and i  think that unfortunately a lot of people  still think that they can have opinions  about climate change we can have  opinions about jazz we can have opinions  about  the timothy chalamet version of dune we  can't have opinions about climate change  because it's climate change is just an  incontrovertible fact  okay can we have an opinion about this  then boris johnson again today in his  speech mentioned scientists in new  zealand who are  working out ways of injecting cows so  that they produce less methane now isn't  that a sensible way to go so meat eaters  can still eat meat but the reduction in  methane means that we're helping the  planet uh i mean not to be dismissive  but it does remind me a little bit of  that old monty python skit where they  dig a trench in the beach for actors to  walk in and then they make the actors  walk on boxes  seems like there might be a simpler way  to address the solution when you're  injecting cows to have them produce less  methane  there might simply be a more direct way  of getting cows to produce less methane  perhaps by having fewer cows because the  other thing  that doesn't take into account is sort  of like  the ancillary carbon footprint of animal  agriculture which is rainforest  deforestation  um water production  moving  livestock to and from slaughterhouses  etc like there's such a huge knock-on  effect so  i i don't mean to make light of it but  it does seem like there might be a more  logical effective way of addressing the  consequences of animal agriculture  okay uh it's been a pleasure speaking to  you and getting your thoughts thank you  very much indeed moby much appreciated  oh thank you for having me
 

